{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"cursed-limitless-cobra","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nA cross-subtree blocking link is silently substituted for library and MCP callers. `rebar link a b depends_on` may record a DIFFERENT edge than the one requested — escalating each endpoint to the nearest common ancestor's children — and the caller is told only `\"ok\"`.\n\nThis is the \"Silent\" impact bug 1803-df54-18bb-4881 reported (\"the user believes T1->S was recorded; it was not\"). Story affe-2b42-4ee4-4e12 fixed *which* edge escalation produces, but not whether the caller is told. It remains unfixed on the path agents actually use.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`add_dependency` composes a machine-readable redirect record (`src/rebar/graph/_links.py:207`) and prints it to stdout via `_emit_redirect` (`src/rebar/graph/_links.py:213`, called at `:269` and `:282`). stdout is the ONLY channel — the record is never returned.\n\nThe CLI sees it. Nothing else does:\n\n- `rebar.link()` calls `composer.link_core(..., quiet=True)` (`src/rebar/_lib_writes.py:615`);\n- `quiet` wraps the call in `contextlib.redirect_stdout(sink), contextlib.redirect_stderr(sink)` (`src/rebar/_commands/composer.py:629`), discarding both the record and the `REDIRECT:` warning;\n- the MCP tool `link_tickets` calls `rebar.link(...)` and returns the literal `\"ok\"` (`src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py:213-214`).\n\nSo an agent linking two tickets over MCP is told the operation succeeded, with no indication that different endpoints were recorded.\n\n## Approach\n\nMake the redirect record a RETURN VALUE, and leave `quiet=True` alone.\n\nRemoving `quiet` is the obvious-looking fix and is wrong: `rebar-mcp` speaks MCP-over-stdio (`src/rebar/mcp_server.py:668`), so an unsuppressed `print()` inside a tool call would inject a bare JSON object into the JSON-RPC stream and corrupt the protocol. The same suppression protects four other in-process callers that must not emit stray stdout — the reconciler's inbound apply (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py:502`), `src/rebar/llm/code_review/finalize.py:106`, and `src/rebar/review_bot/voter.py:343`. `quiet` is load-bearing; the defect is that stdout is the only channel, not that it is suppressed.\n\nThe change is additive at every layer, because all three functions currently return `None`:\n\n1. `add_dependency` returns the redirect record (or `None` when nothing was escalated). Its existing stdout print stays exactly as-is, so the CLI contract pinned by `test_add_dependency_cross_story_emits_redirect_json_to_stdout` (`tests/scripts/graph/test_graph_hierarchy_adddep.py:109`) is untouched.\n2. `link_core` returns what `add_dependency` returned, still inside the `quiet` sink.\n3. `rebar.link()` returns it to library callers.\n4. `link_tickets` returns `\"ok\"` unchanged when nothing was escalated, and when something was, a one-line string naming BOTH pairs — the requested one and the recorded one.\n\nRejected: change `link_tickets` to return a structured dict. Better for machine consumption, but it changes the tool's declared return type from `str`, which moves its MCP `outputSchema` and drags in the schema-drift gate (`scripts/gen_mcp_reference.py`) and the `tests/interfaces/contracts/` schema guards — a contract change disproportionate to making a substitution visible. Every sibling MUTATE tool (`unlink_tickets`, `edit_ticket`) also returns `\"ok\"`, so a string keeps the surface consistent. The string is the minimum that removes the silence; a structured payload can follow if a consumer needs to parse it.\n\nRejected: log a warning instead of returning. `logger.warning` already fires at `src/rebar/graph/_links.py:201` and is swallowed by the same `quiet` sink, so it reaches no one; and a log line is not available to the caller as a value.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/graph/_links.py` — `add_dependency` returns the redirect record (or `None`); the stdout print and its ordering after the durable write are unchanged.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/composer.py` — `link_core` returns what `add_dependency` returned; the `quiet` sink stays.\n- `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py` — `rebar.link()` returns the record to library callers.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py` — `link_tickets` reports the substitution in its return string when one occurred.\n- `tests/scripts/graph/test_graph_hierarchy_adddep.py` — cover the new return value alongside the existing stdout assertion.\n- `tests/unit/test_mcp_writes.py` — cover the MCP tool's two return shapes. If that module does not exist, create it.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Happy path: a sibling link (no escalation) returns `None` from `add_dependency` and `rebar.link()`, and `link_tickets` returns exactly `\"ok\"` — the unchanged shape.\n- Escalation: a cross-subtree link returns a record carrying the original and resolved endpoints, and `link_tickets`' string contains both the requested pair and the recorded pair.\n- Regression: the existing stdout assertion still passes, proving the CLI channel was not moved or removed.\n- Regression: `quiet=True` still suppresses stdout — asserted by capturing stdout around a `rebar.link()` call that escalates and confirming it is empty, which is the property protecting the MCP stdio stream.\n- Edge: a rejected link (redundant / cyclic / closed endpoint) still raises rather than returning a record, so the return value can never be mistaken for success.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `add_dependency` returns the redirect record for an escalated link and `None` for an unescalated one; verified by the new cases in `tests/scripts/graph/test_graph_hierarchy_adddep.py`.\n- [ ] `rebar.link()` returns that same record to library callers; verified by a case asserting the resolved endpoints differ from the requested ones.\n- [ ] The MCP `link_tickets` tool returns exactly `\"ok\"` when nothing was escalated, and a string naming BOTH the requested and the recorded pair when something was; verified by the two cases in `tests/unit/test_mcp_writes.py`.\n- [ ] `rebar.link()` still writes nothing to stdout even when it escalates; verified by capturing stdout around the call and asserting it is empty — the property that keeps the MCP stdio stream intact.\n- [ ] `test_add_dependency_cross_story_emits_redirect_json_to_stdout` passes unmodified; verified by `git diff` showing that test body unchanged.\n- [ ] The MCP tool's declared return type is still `str`, so `docs/mcp-reference.md` needs no regeneration; verified by `python scripts/gen_mcp_reference.py` leaving the file unchanged.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and `pytest tests/scripts/graph/ tests/unit/test_mcp_writes.py` all pass; verified by running each.","id":"fec5-d8bb-86cd-453e","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Cross-subtree link escalation is silent for library and MCP callers"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785442852267266001,"uuid":"90d52e80-8736-4576-ba5c-1c133b7399ca"}